From: Canaan K230 BSP <bsp@canaan-creative.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 15:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] media: canaan: vpu: take MMU pages from the normal allocator

An encode session on a 128 MB board costs about 6 MB of CMA at 720p and
12 MB at 1080p, all of it charged to the 52 MB CMA area that has to be
reserved up front and is useless to everything else while it sits idle.
Almost none of that memory has any reason to be in CMA.

The MVE has its own MMU. mvx_mmu_pages carries an array of physical page
addresses and mvx_mmu_map_pages() installs them into the MVE page table
one at a time, which is the whole point of the structure: the device
sees a contiguous virtual range built out of pages that are scattered in
physical memory. The RTOS port of this same driver allocates them one
page at a time for exactly that reason.

The Linux port does not. mvx_mmu_alloc_pages() and mvx_mmu_resize_pages()
both call mvx_mmu_alloc_contiguous_pages() for the entire region in one
go, so a 720p encoder asking for its reference frames needs ~5 MB of
physically contiguous memory in a single block. The buddy allocator
cannot produce that on a board that has been up for more than a few
minutes, which leaves CMA as the only possible source - not because the
hardware needs contiguity, but because the allocation asked for it.

Allocate page by page instead, and let mvx_mmu_alloc_page() prefer
alloc_page() over asking CMA for a single page - one page is contiguous
by definition, so that call never needed the CMA area either.

The normal allocator is a preference, not a requirement. At 1080p the
firmware asks for about 11 MB through RPC and the normal pool on a
128 MB board cannot supply all of it; the first version of this change
then invoked the OOM killer from the RPC workqueue while 28 MB of CMA
sat unused (free:7500 pages of which free_cma:7171). So when normal
memory is exhausted the allocation falls back to CMA, with __GFP_NORETRY
keeping the failing attempt from reaching the OOM killer on the way
down. The free path validates the PFN range through
dma_release_from_contiguous() - which returns false for pages outside
the device's CMA area without touching them - so pages from either pool
are returned to the pool they came from.

mvx_mmu_alloc_contiguous_pages() stays, with one caller left:
map_fw_print_ram() maps a 512 kB firmware debug buffer and takes a
direct CPU pointer to it with phys_to_virt(), so that one really does
need a contiguous block.

Two consequences worth stating. Total memory use does not change; what
changes is which pool it comes out of, so the CMA reservation can be
sized closer to what the camera and display pipelines alone need - on a
720p product the encoder now costs 0.5 MB of CMA instead of 6 MB. And
the order-6 allocation failures that made the encoder return EIO until
reboot cannot happen on this path any more, since nothing on it asks
for a block larger than one page.

This also fixes a latent bug in the resize path, where a missing pair of
braces left "page += MVE_PAGE_SIZE" outside the inner loop. It was
harmless only because MVX_PAGES_PER_PAGE is 1 when PAGE_SIZE equals
MVE_PAGE_SIZE.

Signed-off-by: Canaan K230 BSP <bsp@canaan-creative.com>
--- a/drivers/media/platform/canaan/vpu/mvx_mmu.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/canaan/vpu/mvx_mmu.c
@@ -618,9 +618,23 @@
 	phys_addr_t pa;
 	dma_addr_t dma_handle;
 
-	page = dma_alloc_from_contiguous(dev, 1, 0, 0);
-	if (page == NULL)
-		return 0;
+	/*
+	 * A single page is contiguous by definition, so the CMA area is not
+	 * needed here. Prefer the normal allocator, which keeps the CMA
+	 * reservation available for the camera and display pipelines, but
+	 * fall back to CMA when normal memory runs out: at 1080p the
+	 * encoder firmware asks for about 11 MB through RPC and on a 128 MB
+	 * board the normal pool cannot supply all of it - with no fallback
+	 * the allocation invokes the OOM killer and takes the board down.
+	 * __GFP_NORETRY keeps the failing attempt from reaching the OOM
+	 * killer on the way down.
+	 */
+	page = alloc_page(GFP_KERNEL | __GFP_NORETRY | __GFP_NOWARN);
+	if (page == NULL) {
+		page = dma_alloc_from_contiguous(dev, 1, 0, 0);
+		if (page == NULL)
+			return 0;
+	}
 
 	dma_handle = dma_map_page(dev, page, 0, PAGE_SIZE, DMA_BIDIRECTIONAL);
 	if (dma_mapping_error(dev, dma_handle) != 0) {
@@ -638,7 +652,7 @@
 	return pa;
 
 free_page:
-	dma_release_from_contiguous(dev, page, 1);
+	__free_page(page);
 	return 0;
 }
 
@@ -697,7 +711,15 @@
 	page = phys_to_page(pa);
 
 	dma_unmap_page(dev, pa, PAGE_SIZE, DMA_BIDIRECTIONAL);
-	dma_release_from_contiguous(dev, page, 1);
+	/*
+	 * The page may come from either pool, depending on where the
+	 * allocation fell back. dma_release_from_contiguous() validates
+	 * the PFN range and returns false for pages outside the device's
+	 * CMA area without touching them, so the same free path handles
+	 * both origins.
+	 */
+	if (!dma_release_from_contiguous(dev, page, 1))
+		__free_page(page);
 }
 
 struct mvx_mmu_pages *mvx_mmu_alloc_pages(struct device *dev, size_t count,
@@ -729,32 +751,37 @@
 	pages->capacity = capacity;
 	INIT_LIST_HEAD(&pages->dmabuf);
 
-	if (count) {
+	/*
+	 * The MVE has its own MMU and these pages are handed to it through
+	 * mvx_mmu_map_pages(), which maps them one by one. Nothing requires
+	 * them to be contiguous with each other, so ask for them one at a
+	 * time. Asking for the whole region as a single block instead means
+	 * only the CMA area can ever satisfy it: a 720p encode session wants
+	 * about 5 MB in one piece, which the buddy allocator cannot produce
+	 * once the system has been up for a while.
+	 */
+	for (pages->count = 0; pages->count < count;) {
+		phys_addr_t page;
+		unsigned int i;
+
 		/*
 		 * Allocate a Linux page. It will typically be of the same size
 		 * as the MVE page, but could also be larger.
 		 */
+		page = mvx_mmu_alloc_page(dev);
+		if (page == 0) {
+			ret = -ENOMEM;
+			goto release_pages;
+		}
 
 		/*
 		 * If the Linux page is larger than the MVE page, then
 		 * we iterate and add physical addresses with an offset from
 		 * the Linux page.
 		 */
-		phys_addr_t page;
-		unsigned int i;
-
-		page = mvx_mmu_alloc_contiguous_pages(dev, count);
-		if (page == 0) {
-			ret = -ENOMEM;
-			goto release_pages;
-		}
-
-		for (pages->count = 0; pages->count < count;) {
-			for (i = 0; i < MVX_PAGES_PER_PAGE; i++) {
-				pages->pages[pages->count++] = page + i * MVE_PAGE_SIZE;
-				page += MVE_PAGE_SIZE;
-			}
-		}
+		for (i = 0; i < MVX_PAGES_PER_PAGE; i++)
+			pages->pages[pages->count++] =
+				page + i * MVE_PAGE_SIZE;
 	}
 
 	return pages;
@@ -934,19 +961,17 @@
 	}
 
 	/* Allocate pages if npage is larger than allocated pages. */
-	if (pages->count < npages) {
+	while (pages->count < npages) {
 		phys_addr_t page;
 		unsigned int i;
 
-		page = mvx_mmu_alloc_contiguous_pages(pages->dev, npages - pages->count);
+		page = mvx_mmu_alloc_page(pages->dev);
 		if (page == 0)
 			return -ENOMEM;
 
-		for (; pages->count < npages;) {
-			for (i = 0; i < MVX_PAGES_PER_PAGE; i++)
-				pages->pages[pages->count++] = page + i * MVE_PAGE_SIZE;
-				page += MVE_PAGE_SIZE;
-		}
+		for (i = 0; i < MVX_PAGES_PER_PAGE; i++)
+			pages->pages[pages->count++] =
+				page + i * MVE_PAGE_SIZE;
 	}
 
 	return remap_pages(pages, oldcount);
